  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Our CWA will remain on the eastern periphery of a \nlarge high centered just east of the Four Corners region. Early \nmorning satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed a shortwave trough \nstretching from the mid Mississippi valley back across the ArkLaTex. \nThis shortwave trough will continue sliding southeast today around \nthe large high and bring low rain chances to our northern zones. Our \nPWATs got knocked down to just below and inch and a half per the 00Z \nSat JAN sounding. With surface ridging across the Gulf states \nshifting south across the northern Gulf by tonight, low level \nmoisture is expected to increase slightly. Local radars were \ncurrently quiet but the chance for rain is expected this morning in \nour northwest and gradually shift east across the Highway 82 corridor \nthrough this afternoon as the shortwave trough swings through. Some \nof the early model guidance was showing the potential heavy rain \njust to our northwest but rainfall amounts in our CWA are not \nexpected to cause any runoff issues. The southern half of our CWA is \nexpected to remain dry with heat and humidity remaining to be the \nmain concern. The combination of temperatures in the 90s and high \nhumidity will result in peak heat index values near 105F over most \nof the area today. /22/\n\nSunday through next Friday...\n\nThe perfect word to describe the forecast for the long term period \nwould be 'HOT'. Dangerous heat stress conditions will be the primary \nfocus for the long term as a strong, zonally oriented upper-level \nridge continues to amplify over the western/central CONUS on Sunday. \nAfternoon highs are forecasted to peak in the mid to upper 90s under \nmostly sunny skies. This, combined with dewpoints in the mid-70s, \nwill allow for peak heat index values in excess of 105 degrees \nacross much of the area. For this reason, an \"Elevated\" risk for \ndangerous heat will be advertised for most our area for Sunday \nincluding portions of southeast AR and all of our northeast LA \nparishes. Likewise, a heat advisory will likely be needed for most \nof our CWA heading into Sunday. \n\nA slight chance for scattered to isolated showers and storms cannot \nbe ruled out for portions of northeast and east MS Sunday afternoon. \nQuiet weather conditions will prevail across our CWA heading into \nSunday night with nighttime lows dipping into the mid 70s. Rain \nchances heading into the new work week will be limited by the ridge \naloft, but a wave may pass through the area mid week and trigger \nsome shower and thunderstorm activity.\n\n\"Elevated\" to \"Significant\" heat stress will continue Monday through \nThursday with temperatures forecasted to be in the upper 90s to near \n100 degrees, along with heat index value readings in the 110 to 115 \nrange areawide. An areawide heat advisory will likely be needed for \nMonday. A heat warning may eventually needed for a good portion of \nthe area by Tuesday. If a heat warning is issued for Tuesday, it \nwill likely start from the Delta and cover areas along and west of I-\n55 including portions of southeast AR and all of our northeast LA \nparishes. As we go through the mid-week and heat stress continue to \nworsen, general thinking is to expand the heat warning further east \nacross portions of southeast MS. As always, at this time of year, \nplease make sure loved ones, neighbors, and pets have a cool place \nto spend the hot afternoons and warm nights. Heat trends will \ncontinue to be closely monitored as we get closer. \n\nScattered showers and storms will start to increase across much of \nthe area heading into Friday. There will be slight higher rain \nchances across the area (30-45%) on Saturday which could help bring \nsome brief relief from the heat. /CR/",
